A guest Wi-Fi poster, and the password it deliberately does not publish

Autumn asked for a QR that puts a device on the Wi-Fi. The generic tool for
making one is going into station-node (tiliv/station-node#59) because it is
useful anywhere; this is the FCPM half, because it is FCPM's network and FCPM
should hold and manage it.

    _data/wifi.yml          the network. Not the password.
    script/make-wifi-qr.py  generates assets/img/wifi-qr.svg, gitignored
    wifi/poster.md          /wifi/poster/ — print it, tape it up
    script/test_no_secrets.py  three new mechanical guards

To use it, once the SSID is confirmed:

    python3 script/make-wifi-qr.py      # prompts for the password

Then print /wifi/poster/. iOS Camera and Android Camera read the `WIFI:` scheme
natively — a guest points a phone at it and is on. Nobody reads a password out
loud across the lobby, and nobody mistypes it.

## The decision this PR does NOT make

**The password is not in this repository and the generated code is gitignored.**

A QR is not encryption. It is a font. `assets/img/wifi-qr.svg` committed here
would publish the guest password to anyone who points a phone at a public
repository, and it would not *look* like publishing a password while you did
it — which is precisely the misreading `test_no_secrets.py` already exists to
make impossible, so it seemed right to extend that file rather than add a new
one beside it.

The distinction is not "who could learn it" — anyone standing in the lobby can
read the poster. It is *how far the lobby reaches.* On the wall, the audience
is people already in the building. On fcpublicmedia.org it is everyone,
forever, including in git history after somebody removes the line.

**If FCPM would rather just publish it, that is a legitimate choice and it is
theirs.** Plenty of venues print the guest password on the menu. It is a small
change — drop the gitignore line, commit the SVG, delete two tests — but it
should be a decision somebody makes on purpose, in a PR that says so, not
something that happens because committing the file was convenient. I have
deliberately not made it either way.

## TODO before this can print anything: confirm the SSID

`_data/wifi.yml` currently guesses **`FC Public WiFi`**, taken from the saved
networks on Autumn's laptop — which also lists `FC Public Media Guest`, and
nobody has said which one guests are meant to use. The laptop's Wi-Fi was off,
so this could not be read from the network itself.

`confirmed: false` gates it, and the generator refuses to run until somebody
who knows sets it true. That is not ceremony: a poster with the wrong SSID
scans perfectly and joins nothing, and there is no symptom to debug from — the
guest just assumes the Wi-Fi is broken. It is the same hazard `check-in/
poster.md` already warns about for its URL.

Two lines to change, both in `_data/wifi.yml`.

## The two silent failures in the format, both handled

`WIFI:` payloads fail by *scanning fine and joining nothing*, which is worse
than failing to scan:

- `\ ; , : "` are structure, not text. An SSID of `Carnegie;Guest` truncates at
  the semicolon and yields a valid code for a network named `Carnegie`.
- An all-hex SSID or password (`deadbeef`, `abc123`) is read as raw bytes
  unless quoted.

The backslash in that first list is the one worth naming, because it looks
redundant and is not — it is the escape character itself. Leaving it out of
the character class is the easy version of this bug, it only shows up on the
one password that contains a backslash, and I wrote it that way first.

## Verified

- **Round-tripped, not eyeballed.** Generated the SVG, rasterised it, and
  decoded it back with the system Vision framework
  (`VNDetectBarcodesRequest`). `WIFI:T:WPA;S:FC Public WiFi;P:…;;` came back
  byte-identical, including a password with a space in it.
- **Payloads cross-checked** against the independently round-trip-verified
  station-node tool, across four cases: WPA, escaped SSID and password, open
  network, all-hex-plus-hidden. All four identical.
- **`bundle exec jekyll build` is clean**, and the poster page was inspected
  rendered, in both states — with the code present and without it. The page is
  `sitemap: false` and does not appear in the built sitemap.
- **`script/test_no_secrets.py`: 12 tests, all passing.** The three new
  guards were each watched to fail on purpose before being left green.
  `script/` is already on the `_config.yml` exclude list, so no change was
  needed there.

## The `_site` guard is the one that earns its keep

The tracked-file checks catch the obvious mistake. The one that would actually
happen is different: CI builds from a clean checkout and so can never have the
code, but README documents `npx wrangler deploy` from a local checkout as the
fallback when Actions is unavailable, and that publishes whatever `_site`
contains. Somebody who printed the poster an hour earlier would ship the
password without touching a single file git can see.

So there is a check for that specifically, and its failure message says what
to do rather than just that something is wrong. It fires only when `_site`
exists, which is exactly when a manual deploy is possible.

## Notes

- `.advocate-engine` is pinned 5 commits behind its `origin/main`. Reported,
  not touched — that is its own PR, by whoever owns it.
- `make-qr.py`'s docstring says it is "the one script here that is not
  standard-library-only". That was already untrue (`build-prices.py` and
  `sync-feeds.py` both import yaml) and this adds a third; `make-wifi-qr.py`
  uses PyYAML rather than the regex trick next door, since the config is
  nested. Left alone rather than corrected in an unrelated diff.
- The password is prompted for rather than taken as an argument, so it stays
  out of shell history and out of `ps` output. `FCPM_WIFI_PASSWORD` is there
  for scripting.
- Nobody has scanned one of these with an actual phone. The decoder says the
  bytes are right; it does not say iOS is happy. Worth one real scan before
  this gets laminated.
